                "content": "# Step-Growth Polymerization with Branching: Complete Solution\n\n## Part (1): Mean Number of Other B Groups on a B Monomer\n\n**Derivation of the formula.** When we follow a bond to arrive at a B functional group on a monomer, we are sampling monomers *proportional to the number of B groups they carry* (size-biased sampling). A monomer of type $i$ with functionality $f_i$ contributes $N_i f_i$ B groups to the denominator. Once we land on such a monomer, the number of *other* B groups available for further branching is $(f_i - 1)$. The expected number of other B groups is therefore:\n\n$$g_B = \\frac{\\sum_i N_i f_i(f_i-1)}{\\sum_i N_i f_i}$$\n\n**Evaluation.** With $N_{B_2} = 9$, $f_2 = 2$ and $N_{B_3} = 1$, $f_3 = 3$:\n\n$$g_B = \\frac{9(2)(1) + 1(3)(2)}{9(2) + 1(3)} = \\frac{18 + 6}{18 + 3} = \\frac{24}{21} = \\frac{8}{7}$$\n\n**Why the simple average is wrong.** The molecular-number average functionality is $\\bar{f} = (9 \\times 2 + 1 \\times 3)/10 = 2.1$, and one might naively use $g_B = \\bar{f} - 1 = 1.1$. But this undercounts: the $B_3$ monomers, though rare by number, contribute 3 out of 21 B groups (14.3%) and each offers 2 other B groups upon arrival. The correct weighting by the number of B groups (not molecules) gives $g_B = 8/7 \\approx 1.143$, which is larger. The discrepancy arises because a randomly chosen B group is more likely to reside on a $B_3$ than the molecular fraction (1/10) would suggest.\n\n---\n\n## Part (2): Flory\u2013Stockmayer Gel Condition\n\n**Branching-process argument.** Track a single B functional group as the \"seed\" of a branch:\n\n1. The B group is **unreacted** with probability $(1-p)$: the branch dies.\n2. The B group is **reacted** with probability $p$: it connects to an A group on an $A_2$ monomer.\n3. That $A_2$ has **one other** A group, which is reacted with probability $p$, leading to a new B group.\n4. That new B group resides on a B monomer carrying, on average, $g_B$ **other** B groups, each of which is independently reacted with probability $p$.\n\nThe **mean number of new B branches** spawned from one existing B branch is therefore:\n\n$$\\sigma = p \\cdot 1 \\cdot p \\cdot g_B = p^2\\, g_B$$\n\nGelation occurs when the expected progeny equals unity (the branching process transitions from subcritical to supercritical):\n\n$$\\boxed{p_A\\, p_B\\, g_B = 1}$$\n\nSince $p_A = p_B = p$ and $g_B = 8/7$:\n\n$$p_c^2 = \\frac{7}{8} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad p_c = \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{8}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{4} \\approx 0.9354$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (3): Stoichiometry and Verification at $p = 0.900$\n\n**Amount of $A_2$ required.**\n\n| Species | Moles | B groups |\n|---------|-------|----------|\n| $B_2$   | 9.00  | 18.00    |\n| $B_3$   | 1.00  | 3.00     |\n| **Total** |      | **21.00** |\n\nFor exact stoichiometry: $N_A = N_B = 21.00\\;\\text{mol}$, so\n\n$$n_{A_2} = \\frac{21.00}{2} = 10.50\\;\\text{mol}$$\n\n**Below or above the gel point?**\n\n$$p^2 g_B = (0.900)^2 \\times \\frac{8}{7} = 0.81 \\times 1.1429 = 0.9257 < 1$$\n\nThe system is **below** the gel point ($p = 0.900 < p_c \\approx 0.9354$).\n\n**Number-average degree of polymerization.** Under the no-cycle (tree) assumption, each bond reduces the cluster count by 1:\n\n$$\\text{Number of bonds} = p \\times N_B = 0.900 \\times 21.00 = 18.90\\;\\text{mol}$$\n\n$$\\text{Number of clusters} = N_{\\text{monomers}} - \\text{bonds} = 20.50 - 18.90 = 1.60\\;\\text{mol}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{X_n = \\frac{20.50}{1.60} = 12.81}$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (4): Extinction-Probability Equations at $p = 0.960$\n\n**Derivation of the coupled equations.**\n\n*For $x_A$:* A randomly chosen A group is unreacted with probability $(1-p)$ (extinction). With probability $p$ it is reacted and connects to a B group on a B monomer. The probability that this monomer is $B_2$ is proportional to its B-group count: $18/21 = 6/7$; for $B_3$: $3/21 = 1/7$.\n\n- On $B_2$: one other B branch must be extinct \u2192 probability $x_B$.\n- On $B_3$: two other B branches must both be extinct \u2192 probability $x_B^2$.\n\n$$x_A = (1-p) + p\\!\\left(\\frac{6}{7}x_B + \\frac{1}{7}x_B^2\\right)$$\n\n*For $x_B$:* A randomly chosen B group is unreacted with probability $(1-p)$. With probability $p$ it connects to an A group on an $A_2$, which has one other A branch that must be extinct:\n\n$$x_B = (1-p) + p\\,x_A$$\n\n**Solving for $p = 0.960$** (so $1-p = 0.04$):\n\nSubstituting $x_B = 0.04 + 0.96\\,x_A$ into the first equation and collecting terms yields the quadratic:\n\n$$13824\\,x_A^2 - 21823\\,x_A + 7999 = 0$$\n\nSince $x_A = 1$ is one root (verified: $13824 - 21823 + 7999 = 0$), the other root is:\n\n$$x_A = \\frac{7999}{13824} \\approx 0.5786$$\n\nThen:\n\n$$x_B = 0.04 + 0.96 \\times \\frac{7999}{13824} = \\frac{343}{576} \\approx 0.5955$$\n\n**Verification:**\n$$x_A = 0.04 + 0.96\\!\\left(\\tfrac{6}{7}\\cdot\\tfrac{343}{576} + \\tfrac{1}{7}\\cdot\\tfrac{343}{576}\\right)^{\\!\\!2}\\bigg|_{\\text{computed}} = \\frac{7999}{13824}\\;\\checkmark$$\n\n$$\\boxed{x_A = \\frac{7999}{13824} \\approx 0.5786, \\qquad x_B = \\frac{343}{576} \\approx 0.5955}$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (5): Fraction of Molecules in the Infinite Gel\n\nA molecule is in the **sol** if and only if *all* of its functional-group branches are extinct:\n\n| Molecule | Moles | Sol condition | Sol probability |\n|----------|-------|---------------|-----------------|\n| $A_2$   | 10.50 | both A branches extinct | $x_A^2$ |\n| $B_2$   | 9.00  | both B branches extinct | $x_B^2$ |\n| $B_3$   | 1.00  | all three B branches extinct | $x_B^3$ |\n\n**Moles in the sol:**\n\n$$n_{\\text{sol}} = 10.50\\,x_A^2 + 9.00\\,x_B^2 + 1.00\\,x_B^3$$\n\nNumerically:\n$$x_A^2 = 0.5786^2 = 0.3348, \\quad x_B^2 = 0.5955^2 = 0.3546, \\quad x_B^3 = 0.5955^3 = 0.2112$$\n\n$$n_{\\text{sol}} = 10.50(0.3348) + 9.00(0.3546) + 1.00(0.2112) = 3.515 + 3.191 + 0.211 = 6.918\\;\\text{mol}$$\n\n**Fraction in the gel:**\n\n$$w_{\\text{gel}} = 1 - \\frac{n_{\\text{sol}}}{n_{\\text{total}}} = 1 - \\frac{6.918}{20.50}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{w_{\\text{gel}} \\approx 0.6625 \\approx 66.3\\%}$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (6): Why the Nontrivial Solution Is Physical Above $p_c$\n\nThe map governing the extinction probability of a B-branch (after eliminating $x_A$) is:\n\n$$F(x) = 1 - p + p\\!\\left[\\frac{6}{7}(1-p+px) + \\frac{1}{7}(1-p+px)^2\\right]$$\n\nThe extinction probability is the **smallest non-negative fixed point** of $F$, a standard theorem in the theory of Galton\u2013Watson branching processes.\n\n**Stability analysis at $x = 1$:**\n\n$$F'(x) = p^2\\!\\left(\\frac{6}{7} + \\frac{2}{7}(1 - p + px)\\right)$$\n\n$$F'(1) = p^2\\!\\left(\\frac{6}{7} + \\frac{2}{7}\\right) = p^2\\, g_B$$\n\n| Regime | $F'(1) = p^2 g_B$ | Fixed points in $[0,1]$ | Physical solution |\n|--------|:-----------------:|:-----------------------:|:-----------------:|\n| $p < p_c$ | $< 1$ | Only $x = 1$ | $x = 1$ (certain extinction, no gel) |\n| $p = p_c$ | $= 1$ | $x = 1$ (bifurcation point) | $x = 1$ (marginal) |\n| $p > p_c$ | $> 1$ | $x = 1$ **and** $x^* < 1$ | $x^*$ (partial extinction, gel exists) |\n\n- **Below $p_c$:** $F'(1) < 1$ makes $x = 1$ the unique and stable fixed point. All branches are certain to die out \u2014 no gel forms.\n\n- **Above $p_c$:** $F'(1) > 1$ makes $x = 1$ an **unstable** fixed point (a small perturbation below 1 is iterated toward $x^*$, not back to 1). The new fixed point $x^* < 1$ is stable and represents the true extinction probability. Physically, a nonzero fraction of branches survive to infinity, forming the gel.\n\n- **At $p = p_c$:** The bifurcation occurs. The derivative equals unity, the two fixed points coalesce, and the gel fraction is zero. This is precisely the Flory\u2013Stockmayer criterion $p^2 g_B = 1$ derived in Part (2).\n\nThus, the $x_A = x_B = 1$ solution, while mathematically valid, corresponds to an **unstable** (non-physical) branch of solutions above the critical point. The nontrivial solution $x^* < 1$ is the one that the branching process actually converges to, encoding the finite (but nonzero) probability that a given branch terminates \u2014 and hence the existence of an infinite gel network.",
