functional group, there's 1 other A functional group on the same A\u2082 monomer.\n- That other A functional group has reacted with probability p, leading to a B functional group.\n- That B functional group leads to its parent B monomer, which has g_B other B functional groups (on average).\n- Each of those has reacted with probability p.\n\nSo the \"reproduction number\" (expected number of new branches from one existing branch) is:\n\u03c3 = p \u00d7 1 \u00d7 p \u00d7 g_B = p\u00b2 g_B\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The branching process:\n\nActually, let me think of it differently. We're tracking B functional groups.\n\nStart: A B functional group is selected.\n- With probability (1-p), it's unreacted \u2192 no new branches (the chain stops)\n- With probability p, it's reacted \u2192 it's connected to an A functional group on an A\u2082 monomer\n  - The A\u2082 monomer has 1 other A functional group\n  - That other A functional group is reacted with probability p \u2192 leads to a B functional group\n    - That B functional group is on a B monomer which has g_B other B functional groups\n    - Each of those g_B other B functional groups is reacted with probability p \u2192 each leads to an A functional group\n\nSo the number of new B functional groups generated from one B functional group is:\n- 0 with probability (1-p)\n- If reacted (prob p): leads to 1 A on A\u2082, which has 1 other A, which is reacted with prob p, leading to 1 B, which is on a monomer with g_B other B's, each reacted with prob p.\n\nSo expected number of new branches = p \u00d7 p \u00d7 g_B = p\u00b2 g_B\n\nThe gel condition is that this equals 1: p\u00b2 g_B = 1\n\nSince p_A = p_B = p, the condition is p_A p_B g_B = p\u00b2 g_B = 1.\n\nWait, but the problem states the condition is p_A p_B g_B = 1. Since p_A = p_B = p, this gives p\u00b2 g_B = 1.\n\nSo p_c = 1/\u221a(g_B) = 1/\u221a(8/7) = \u221a(7/8) = \u221a7/(2\u221a2) = \u221a14/4\n\nLet me compute: 7/8 = 0.875, \u221a0.875 \u2248 0.9354\n\nSo p_c \u2248 0.9354.\n\nLet me be more precise: p_c = \u221a(7/8) = \u221a7/(2\u221a2) = \u221a14/4\n\n\u221a14 \u2248 3.7417, so p_c \u2248 3.7417/4 \u2248 0.9354\n\n**Part (3): Amount of A\u2082 and verification at p = 0.900**\n\nB\u2082: 9.00 mol \u2192 18.00 mol of B functional groups\nB\u2083: 1.00 mol \u2192 3.00 mol of B functional groups\nTotal B functional groups = 21.00 mol\n\nFor exact stoichiometry: A functional groups = B functional groups = 21.00 mol\nA\u2082 has 2 A groups, so we need 21.00/2 = 10.50 mol of A\u2082\n\nAt p = 0.900:\np\u00b2 g_B = 0.81 \u00d7 8/7 = 6.48/7 = 0.9257 < 1\n\nSo the system is below the gel point. Good.\n\nNow for X_n at p = 0.900:\n\nX_n = (initial number of monomer molecules) / (number of connected molecular clusters)\n\nInitial number of monomer molecules = 10.50 + 9.00 + 1.00 = 20.50 mol\n\nNumber of connected clusters = initial number of molecules - number of bonds formed\n\nNumber of bonds formed = p \u00d7 (total A functional groups) = 0.9 \u00d7 21.00 = 18.90 mol\n\nWait, actually each bond connects two functional groups (one A and one B). The number of bonds = p \u00d7 N_A = p \u00d7 N_B (since they're stoichiometric).\n\nNumber of bonds = 0.9 \u00d7 21.00 = 18.90 mol\n\nNumber of clusters = 20.50 - 18.90 = 1.60 mol\n\nX_n = 20.50/1.60 = 12.8125\n\nHmm, let me double-check. In a system where we start with N molecules and form B bonds (each bond connecting two molecules), the number of clusters is N - B (assuming no cycles, which is given).\n\nSo X_n = 20.50/(20.50 - 18.90) = 20.50/1.60 = 12.8125\n\n**Part (4): Coupled extinction probability equations**\n\nx_A = probability that following a randomly chosen A functional group away from its parent monomer does not lead to the infinite gel.\n\nx_B = probability that following a randomly chosen B functional group away from its parent monomer does not lead to the infinite gel.\n\nFor x_A:\n- With probability (1-p), the A group is unreacted \u2192 extinction (contribution