e do long division:\n7999.0000/13824\n= 0.5786...\n\n13824 \u00d7 0.57 = 7879.68\n7999 - 7879.68 = 119.32\n119.32/13824 = 0.008632...\n\nSo x_A \u2248 0.578632...\n\nLet me get more digits:\n13824 \u00d7 0.578 = 7990.272\n7999 - 7990.272 = 8.728\n8.728/13824 = 0.000631...\n\nx_A \u2248 0.578631\n\n13824 \u00d7 0.5786 = 7998.5664\n7999 - 7998.5664 = 0.4336\n0.4336/13824 = 0.0000314\n\nx_A \u2248 0.578631\n\nSo x_A = 7999/13824 \u2248 0.5786\n\nx_B = 1/25 + (24/25)(7999/13824) = 1/25 + 24 \u00d7 7999/(25 \u00d7 13824) = 1/25 + 191976/345600\n\nLet me simplify: 24 \u00d7 7999 = 191976\n25 \u00d7 13824 = 345600\n\n191976/345600 = ?\nLet me divide both by 24: 191976/24 = 7999, 345600/24 = 14400\nSo = 7999/14400\n\nx_B = 1/25 + 7999/14400 = 14400/(25 \u00d7 14400) + 7999/(14400 \u00d7 25) \u00d7 25\n= 14400/360000 + 7999/360000 \u00d7 25/25\n\nWait, let me just find common denominator.\n1/25 = 14400/360000... no.\n1/25 = 576/14400\n\nx_B = 576/14400 + 7999/14400 = 8575/14400\n\nSimplify: 8575/14400. GCD?\n8575 = 5 \u00d7 1715 = 5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 343 = 25 \u00d7 343 = 25 \u00d7 7\u00b3\n14400 = 2\u2076 \u00d7 3\u00b2 \u00d7 5\u00b2\n\nGCD = 25\n8575/25 = 343\n14400/25 = 576\n\nSo x_B = 343/576\n\nLet me verify: 343/576 = 0.59548...\n\nLet me check: 343/576\n576 \u00d7 0.5 = 288\n343 - 288 = 55\n55/576 = 0.09548...\nSo x_B \u2248 0.59548\n\nNow let me verify the original equations:\nx_A = 7999/13824 \u2248 0.57863\nx_B = 343/576 \u2248 0.59548\n\nCheck: x_B = 0.04 + 0.96 \u00d7 x_A = 0.04 + 0.96 \u00d7 0.57863 = 0.04 + 0.55548 = 0.59548 \u2713\n\nCheck: x_A = 0.04 + 0.96[(6/7)(343/576) + (1/7)(343/576)\u00b2]\n= 0.04 + 0.96[(6/7)(343/576) + (1/7)(117649/331776)]\n\n(6/7)(343/576) = 2058/4032 = 1029/2016 = 343/672\n\n(1/7)(117649/331776) = 117649/2322432\n\n343/672 + 117649/2322432\n\nLet me find common denominator. 672 = 2\u2075 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 7, 2322432 = ?\n2322432/672 = 3456. So 2322432 = 672 \u00d7 3456.\n\n343/672 = 343 \u00d7 3456/2322432 = 1185408/2322432\n\n1185408 + 117649 = 1303057\n\nSo bracket = 1303057/2322432\n\nx_A = 0.04 + 0.96 \u00d7 1303057/2322432\n= 0.04 + 1250934.72/2322432\n\nHmm, this is getting messy. Let me just use the exact fractions.\n\nx_A = 1/25 + (24/25) \u00d7 1303057/2322432\n\n24 \u00d7 1303057 = 31273368\n25 \u00d7 2322432 = 58060800\n\nx_A = 1/25 + 31273368/58060800\n= 2322432/58060800 + 31273368/58060800\n= 33595800/58060800\n\nLet me simplify: 33595800/58060800\nDivide by 100: 335958/580608\nDivide by 6: 55993/96768\n\nHmm, let me check if 7999/13824 = 55993/96768\n7999 \u00d7 96768 = ?\n13824 \u00d7 55993 = ?\n\nActually, 96768/13824 = 7\nSo 7999/13824 = 7999 \u00d7 7/(13824 \u00d7 7) = 55993/96768 \u2713\n\nSo x_A = 55993/96768 = 7999/13824 \u2713\n\nGreat, so the exact solutions are:\nx_A = 7999/13824 \u2248 0.5786\nx_B = 343/576 \u2248 0.5955\n\n**Part (5): Fraction of monomer molecules in the infinite gel**\n\nA\u2082 molecule is in the sol if both A branches are extinct: probability x_A\u00b2\nB\u2082 molecule is in the sol if both B branches are extinct: probability x_B\u00b2\nB\u2083 molecule is in the sol if all three B branches are extinct: probability x_B\u00b3\n\nFraction in sol:\n- A\u2082: 10.50 \u00d7 x_A\u00b2\n- B\u2082: 9.00 \u00d7 x_B\u00b2\n- B\u2083: 1.00 \u00d7 x_B\u00b3\n\nTotal in sol = 10.50 \u00d7 x_A\u00b2 + 9.00 \u00d7 x_B\u00b2 + 1.00 \u00d7 x_B\u00b3\n\nTotal molecules = 20.50\n\nFraction in gel = 1 - (total in sol)/20.50\n\nLet me compute:\nx_A\u00b2 = (7999/13824)\u00b2 = 63984001/191102976 \u2248 0.33481\nx_B\u00b2 = (343/576)\u00b2 = 117649/331776 \u2248 0.35459\nx_B\u00b3 = (343/576)\u00b3 = 40353607/191102976 \u2248 0.21116\n\nWait, let me compute more carefully.\nx_B = 343/576\nx_B\u00b2 = 117649/331776\nx_B\u00b3 = 117649 \u00d7 343/(331776 \u00d7 576) = 40353607/191102976\n\nx_A = 7999/13824\nx_A\u00b2 = 63984001/191102976\n\nTotal in sol = 10.50 \u00d7 63984001/191102976 + 9.00 \u00d7 117649/331776 + 1.00 \u00d7 40353607/191102976\n\nLet me convert everything to denominator 191102976:\n331776 \u00d7 576 = 191102976? Let me check: 331776 \u00d7 576\n331776 \u00d7 500 = 165888000\n331776 \u00d7 76 = 25214976\nTotal = 191102976 \u2713\n\nSo: 9.00 \u00d7 117649/331776 = 9.00 \u00d7 117649 \u00d7 576/191102976 = 9 \u00d7 67765824/191102976\n\nWait: 117649 \u00d7 576 = ?\n117649 \u00d7 500 = 58824500\n117649 \u00d7 76 = 8941324\nTotal = 67765824\n\nSo 9.00 \u00d7 117649/331776 = 9 \u00d7 67765824/191102976 = 609892416/191102976\n\n10.50 \u00d7 63984001/191102976 = 671832010.5/191102976\n\n1.00 \u00d7 40353607/191102976 = 40353607/191102976\n\nTotal in sol = (671832010.5 + 609892416 + 40353607)/191102976\n= 1322078033.5/191102976\n\u2248 6.918...\n\nWait, that can't be right because the total number of molecules is only 20.50. Let me recheck.\n\nOh wait, I think I messed up. Let me redo.\n\nTotal in sol (in moles) = 10.50 \u00d7 x_A\u00b2 + 9.00 \u00d7 x_B\u00b2 + 1.00 \u00d7 x_B\u00b3\n\nx_A\u00b2 \u2248 0.5786\u00b2 = 0.3348\nx_B\u00b2 \u2248 0.5955\u00b2 = 0.3546\nx_B\u00b3 \u2248 0.5955\u00b3 = 0.2112\n\nTotal in sol \u2248 10.50 \u00d7 0.3348 + 9.00 \u00d7 0.3546 + 1.00 \u00d7 0.2112\n= 3.5154 + 3.1914 + 0.2112\n= 6.9180\n\nFraction in gel = 1 - 6.9180/20.50 = 1 - 0.33746 = 0.66254\n\nSo about 66.3% of monomer molecules are in the gel.\n\nLet me be more precise:\nx_A = 7999/13824\nx_A\u00b2 = 63984001/191102976\n\nLet me compute 10.5 \u00d7 63984001/191102976:\n= 671832010.5/191102976\n\u2248 3.5154\n\nx_B = 343/576\nx_B\u00b2 = 117649/331776\nx_B\u00b3 = 40353607/191102976\n\n9 \u00d7 117649/331776 = 1058841/331776 \u2248 3.1915\n1 \u00d7 40353607/191102976 \u2248 0.21116\n\nTotal in sol \u2248 3.5154 + 3.1915 + 0.2112 = 6.9181\n\nFraction in gel = 1 - 6.9181/20.50 = 1 - 0.33747 = 0.66253\n\nSo approximately 66.25% of molecules are in the gel.\n\nLet me compute this more precisely.\n\n10.5 \u00d7 x_A\u00b2 = 10.5 \u00d7 (7999/13824)\u00b2\n\n(7999/13824)\u00b2 = 7999\u00b2/13824\u00b2 = 63984001/191102976\n\n10.5 \u00d7 63984001/191102976 = 671832010.5/191102976\n\n9 \u00d7 x_B\u00b2 = 9 \u00d7 (343/576)\u00b2 = 9 \u00d7 117649/331776 = 1058841/331776\n\nx_B\u00b3 = (343/576)\u00b3 = 40353607/191102976\n\nTo add these, let me convert to a common denominator. The LCM of 191102976 and 331776:\n191102976 = 331776 \u00d7 576 (as we verified)\nSo common denominator is 191102976.\n\n9 \u00d7 117649/331776 = 9 \u00d7 117649 \u00d7 576/191102976 = 9 \u00d7 67765824/191102976 = 609892416/191102976\n\nTotal in sol = (671832010.5 + 609892416 + 40353607)/191102976\n= 1322078033.5/191102976\n\nHmm, the .5 is because of the 10.5. Let me use fractions.\n\n10.5 = 21/2\n\n21/2 \u00d7 63984001/191102976 = 21 \u00d7 63984001/(2 \u00d7 191102976) = 1343664021/382205952\n\n9 \u00d7 117649/331776 = 9 \u00d7 117649 \u00d7 576/191102976 = 609892416/191102976 = 1219784832/382205952\n\n40353607/191102976 = 80707214/382205952\n\nTotal in sol = (1343664021 + 1219784832 + 80707214)/382205952\n= 2644156067/382205952\n\nFraction in gel = 1 - 2644156067/(382205952 \u00d7 20.5)\n= 1 - 2644156067/(382205952 \u00d7 41/2)\n= 1 - 2 \u00d7 2644156067/(382205952 \u00d7 41)\n= 1 - 5288312134/15670444032\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me just use decimal approximations.\n\nx_A = 7999/13824 = 0.578631...\nx_A\u00b2 = 0.334814...\nx_B = 343/576 = 0.595486...\nx_B\u00b2 = 0.354604...\nx_B\u00b3 = 0.211164...\n\nTotal in sol = 10.5(0.334814) + 9(0.354604) + 1(0.211164)\n= 3.515547 + 3.191436 + 0.211164\n= 6.918147\n\nFraction in gel = 1 - 6.918147/20.50 = 1 - 0.337471 = 0.662529\n\nSo about 66.25% of molecules are in the gel.\n\nActually, let me double-check x_A more precisely.\n7999/13824:\n7999/13824 = 0.57863...\n\nLet me do this division:\n13824 \u00d7 0.57 = 7879.68\n7999 - 7879.68 = 119.32\n119.32/13824 = 0.008632...\n13824 \u00d7 0.008 = 110.592\n119.32 - 110.592 = 8.728\n8.728/13824 = 0.000631...\n13824 \u00d7 0.0006 = 8.2944\n8.728 - 8.2944 = 0.4336\n0.4336/13824 = 0.0000314\n\nSo x_A \u2248 0.578631\n\nx_A\u00b2 = 0.578631\u00b2 = 0.334814 (let me verify: 0.5786\u00b2 = 0.334778, 0.57863\u00b2 \u2248 0.334813)\n\nx_B = 343/576:\n576 \u00d7 0.59 = 339.84\n343 - 339.84 = 3.16\n3.16/576 = 0.005486...\nSo x_B \u2248 0.595486\n\nx_B\u00b2 = 0.595486\u00b2 \u2248 0.354604\nx_B\u00b3 = 0.595486 \u00d7 0.354604 \u2248 0.211164\n\nThese look right.\n\n**Part (6): Explanation of why the nontrivial solution is physical**\n\nThe x_A = x_B = 1 solution corresponds to the case where all branches are extinct, i.e., no gel exists. This is the correct solution below the gel point (p < p_c), where the only solution in [0,1] is the trivial one.\n\nAbove the gel point (p > p_c), both solutions exist. The x_A = x_B = 1 solution still mathematically satisfies the equations, but it's no longer the physical one. The physical interpretation is:\n\nIn a branching process, the extinction probability is the smallest non-negative fixed point of the generating function. Below the critical point, the only fixed point in [0,1] is 1 (certain extinction). Above the critical point, there are two fixed points in [0,1]: one at 1 and one at a value less than 1. The extinction probability is the smaller one because:\n\n1. The branching process starts with a single individual (branch). The probability of ultimate extinction is the smallest fixed point of the generating function (this is a standard result from branching process theory).\n\n2. Physically, above the gel point, there IS a nonzero probability that a branch leads to the infinite gel. So the extinction probability must be less than 1.\n\n3. The stability argument: Below p_c, the derivative of the generating function at x = 1 is less than 1, so x = 1 is the stable fixed point (attractor). Above p_c, the derivative at x = 1 exceeds 1, making x = 1 an unstable fixed point, and the nontrivial solution becomes the stable one.\n\nLet me formalize this. Consider the map f(x) = 1-p + p \u00d7 G(x) where G is the appropriate generating function. The extinction probability is the smallest fixed point of f. \n\nFor the B-branch: x_B = 1-p + px_A, and for the A-branch: x_A = 1-p + p(6/7 x_B + 1/7 x_B\u00b2)\n\nThe combined map is F(x) = 1-p + p(6/7(1-p+px) + 1/7(1-p+px)\u00b2)\n\nF'(x) = p(6/7 \u00d7 p + 1/7 \u00d7 2(1-p+px) \u00d7 p) = p\u00b2(6/7 + 2(1-p+px)/7)\n\nAt x = 1: F'(1) = p\u00b2(6/7 + 2(1)/7) = p\u00b2(8/7) = p\u00b2g_B\n\nSo F'(1) = p\u00b2g_B. The critical point is where F'(1) = 1, i.e., p\u00b2g_B = 1, which is exactly the gel condition.\n\n- Below p_c: F'(1) < 1, so x = 1 is the unique fixed point in [0,1] and it's stable.\n- Above p_c: F'(1) > 1, so x = 1 is unstable, and there's a new fixed point x* < 1 which is stable. This x* is the physical extinction probability.\n\nThis connects directly to the gel point criterion: at p = p_c, F'(1) = 1, and for p > p_c, the trivial solution becomes unstable and the nontrivial solution (representing the existence of the gel) becomes the physical answer.\n\nNow let me put together a complete, well-organized answer.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider part (3). The problem says \"verify whether the system is below or above the gel point\" at p = 0.900.\n\np_c = \u221a(7/8) = \u221a0.875 \u2248 0.93542\n\nSince p = 0.900 < p_c \u2248 0.9354, the system is below the gel point. \u2713\n\nAnd for X_n:\nInitial molecules = 10.50 + 9.00 + 1.00 = 20.50 mol\nNumber of bonds formed = p \u00d7 (number of A groups) = 0.9 \u00d7 21.00 = 18.90 mol (each bond uses one A and one B group)\nNumber of clusters = 20.50 - 18.90 = 1.60 mol (no cycles)\nX_n = 20.50/1.60 = 12.8125\n\nLet me double-check: In a tree (no cycles), if we start with N vertices and form B edges, the number of connected components is N - B. This is because each edge that connects two different components reduces the number of components by 1. In a tree, all edges connect different components (no cycles), so the number of components is N - B.\n\nYes, X_n = 20.50/1.60 = 12.8125.\n\nNow let me write up the full